This error is actually one of the easier ones to fix. The important part is:

Error: Failed to lookup view "505" in views directory
Express is telling you:

"I was asked to render a view called 505.ejs, but I can't find it."

Step 1: Check your global error handler
You probably have something like this near the bottom of app.js:

app.use((err, req, res, next) => {

    console.error(err.stack);

    res.status(500).render("505");
});
or

res.render("505");
Step 2: Check your Views folder
Do you actually have

views/
    505.ejs
?

From our previous conversations, I remember we discussed creating:

views/
    403.ejs
    404.ejs
    500.ejs
Notice the last file is 500.ejs, not 505.ejs.

A 505 status code means:

HTTP Version Not Supported

It isn't the standard page for unexpected server errors.

For application errors, Express uses 500 Internal Server Error.

Step 3: Correct your error handler
It should be:

app.use((err, req, res, next) => {

    console.error(err.stack);

    res.status(500).render("500", {
        pageTitle: "Server Error",
        error: err
    });

});
and make sure you have

views/
    500.ejs
Step 4: Your 404 middleware
Just before the error handler you should have something like:

app.use((req, res) => {

    res.status(404).render("404", {
        pageTitle: "Page Not Found"
    });

});
The order matters
Your app.js should end like this:

// Routes
app.use("/", homeRoutes);
app.use("/auth", authRoutes);
app.use("/predictions", predictionRoutes);
app.use("/admin", adminRoutes);
app.use("/premium", premiumRoutes);

// 404
app.use((req, res) => {
    res.status(404).render("404");
});

// Global Error Handler
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
    console.error(err.stack);

    res.status(500).render("500", {
        error: err
    });
});
