Performance + Scalability

Right now you have:

const [
    ...
] = await Promise.all([
    ...
]);

const recentUsers = await User.find()
    .sort({ createdAt: -1 })
    .limit(5);
That means:

Promise.all waits...

Then MongoDB performs another query.

So you're making the last query sequentially.

The better approach
Simply include it inside the Promise.all().

Add it here:

const [
    totalPredictions,
    premiumPredictions,
    freePredictions,
    pendingPredictions,
    wonPredictions,
    lostPredictions,
    voidPredictions,
    expiredPredictions,
    totalUsers,
    premiumUsers,
    latestPredictions,
    recentUsers
] = await Promise.all([
Then, at the bottom of the array:

Prediction.find()
    .sort({ kickoffTime: -1 })
    .limit(5)
    .populate("createdBy", "username"),

User.find()
    .sort({ createdAt: -1 })
    .limit(5)
Now remove this completely:

const recentUsers = await User.find()
    .sort({ createdAt: -1 })
    .limit(5);
because it's already inside Promise.all().

Why is this better?
Instead of

Query 1
Query 2
...
Query 11

WAIT

Query 12
MongoDB now does

Query 1
Query 2
Query 3
...
Query 12
All together.

That is exactly why we started using Promise.all() in the first place.

⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐ One more recommendation
Since we're already thinking about scalability, I'd also make this small optimization:

User.find()
    .select("username email role subscriptionStatus createdAt")
    .sort({ createdAt: -1 })
    .limit(5)
Instead of fetching the entire user document (including hashed passwords and any future fields), you're only retrieving the columns the dashboard actually needs.

That saves memory and bandwidth and is a good habit to build.

